window.__labLoaded({"id":"0.1","title":"What Is Statistics?","goal":"Summarise one class's 30 test scores two ways: describe the class you have, then infer a plausible range for the wider group.","question":"A maths teacher has the end-of-term scores of her 30 students on her desk. She has two questions: how did my class do, and what can I say about similar students I never tested? We have the 30 scores, and we will answer the first question by describing and the second by inferring.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the 30 test scores","text":"The teacher's 30 end-of-term scores, held in a NumPy array. The mean is 74 and the standard deviation about 12.3, as in the lesson.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nscores = np.array([41,57,61,62,62,62,65,65,68,68,70,72,72,73,73,75,75,76,77,77,78,79,80,84,86,86,88,95,95,98])\n\nprint('students:', len(scores))\nprint('lowest to highest:', scores.min(), 'to', scores.max())","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, under the short name np so we can type np. instead of numpy.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; we need them in step 3 for the confidence interval.","Store the 30 scores in an array, so one command can work on all of them at once.","Print how many scores we have. Always check n first, because everything later depends on it.","Print the lowest and highest score, to see how far the results stretch before we compute anything."],"meaning":"30 students, with scores from 41 to 98. That is a wide spread, so one average will hide differences between students. This is why we measure the spread as well as the centre in the next step.","out":"students: 30\nlowest to highest: 41 to 98","figs":[]},{"title":"Describe: summarise what we have","text":"Descriptive statistics only talk about these 30 students. The mean and median give the centre, and the standard deviation (ddof=1, the sample version) gives the typical distance from the average.","code":"mean = scores.mean()\nmedian = np.median(scores)\nsd = scores.std(ddof=1)\n\nprint(f'mean   = {mean:.1f}')\nprint(f'median = {median:.1f}')\nprint(f'sd     = {sd:.1f}')\nprint(f'about 2/3 of scores lie within {mean - sd:.1f} to {mean + sd:.1f}')","explain":["The mean (add all scores, divide by 30) is the balance point of the class and our headline answer to 'how did we do?'.","The median is the middle score after sorting. We compute it too, to see whether a few extreme scores are pulling the mean.","Standard deviation, the typical distance of a score from the mean. ddof=1 divides by n - 1, because a sample under-estimates the spread of its whole group.","Print the mean. The f-string puts the number inside the text, and :.1f rounds it to one decimal place.","Print the median on the next line so we can compare it with the mean.","Print the standard deviation, the number that says how far scores typically sit from the average.","Mean plus and minus one SD marks the range where about two thirds of bell-shaped scores fall: a picture of a typical student."],"meaning":"The mean and the median are both 74.0, so the class is balanced and no extreme scores drag the average. The SD of 12.3 means a typical student sits about 12 points from 74, and about two thirds of the scores fall between 61.7 and 86.3. These are plain facts about these 30 students.","out":"mean   = 74.0\nmedian = 74.0\nsd     = 12.3\nabout 2/3 of scores lie within 61.7 to 86.3","figs":[]},{"title":"Infer: a 95% confidence interval","text":"Now treat the 30 students as a sample from a bigger group. stats.t.interval gives a range of plausible values for that group's true mean. The standard error (sd divided by the square root of n) says how much a mean of 30 scores wobbles.","code":"n = len(scores)\nse = sd / np.sqrt(n)\n\nlow, high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=mean, scale=se)\n\nprint(f'standard error = {se:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% CI for the true mean: ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')","explain":["Store n, the number of scores. len() counts them, and we need n for both the standard error and the degrees of freedom.","Standard error = sd / square root of n. It says how much a mean of 30 scores would wobble from one sample to the next.","stats.t.interval builds a 95% range for the true mean. It uses the t distribution because we estimated the spread from the sample, with df = n - 1.","Print the standard error to two decimals, so we can see how precise our mean is.","Print the two ends of the interval: the low and high values of the true mean that fit our data."],"meaning":"The standard error is 2.24, so a mean of 30 scores like these would typically wobble by about 2 points. The 95% interval is 69.4 to 78.6. If these students are a fair sample of a wider group, that group's true average plausibly lies in this range. The 74.0 is only our best single guess.","out":"standard error = 2.24\n95% CI for the true mean: (69.4, 78.6)","figs":[]},{"title":"What each one tells you","text":"The describing numbers are exact facts about these 30 scores. The interval is an estimate about people we did not test, and it comes with stated uncertainty. That is the whole difference between the two branches.","code":"print('DESCRIBE: this class averaged', round(mean, 1), 'with a typical spread of', round(sd, 1))\nprint('INFER: a wider group of similar students probably averages between', round(low, 1), 'and', round(high, 1))\nprint('Is the interval narrower than the spread of scores?', (high - low) < 2 * sd)","explain":["Print the descriptive statement. It is a fact about these 30 students only, so it carries no uncertainty.","Print the inferential statement. It is about students we did not test, so we give a range instead of one number.","Compare the interval's width with 2 SDs. The interval is much narrower, because it locates the average, not individual students."],"meaning":"True: the interval is about 9 points wide, far narrower than the roughly 25 points over which individual scores spread. Do not mix them up. The standard deviation describes how students differ, while the interval says where the group's average probably lies.","out":"DESCRIBE: this class averaged 74.0 with a typical spread of 12.3\nINFER: a wider group of similar students probably averages between 69.4 and 78.6\nIs the interval narrower than the spread of scores? True","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"scores <- c(41,57,61,62,62,62,65,65,68,68,70,72,72,73,73,75,75,76,77,77,78,79,80,84,86,86,88,95,95,98)\nmean(scores)\nmedian(scores)\nsd(scores)\nt.test(scores)$conf.int","explain":["Store the 30 scores in a vector called scores. c() is R's way of combining values into one list.","mean() adds the scores and divides by 30, giving the class average.","median() gives the middle score after sorting, a check against extreme scores.","sd() is the sample standard deviation (it divides by n - 1), the typical distance from the mean.","t.test(scores) runs a t-test, but we only want $conf.int from it: the 95% interval for the true mean."],"expect":"mean 74; median 74; sd 12.29; 95 percent confidence interval: 69.41 to 78.59","read":"Four results appear: 74, 74 and 12.29 describe this class (mean, median, SD). The last line prints the interval 69.41 to 78.59, followed by attr(,\"conf.level\") 0.95. That interval is the inference about the wider group.","note":"sd() in R is the sample version (divides by n - 1), the same as ddof=1. t.test(scores) is used here only to get the 95% interval for the mean."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n41 57 61 62 62 62 65 65 68 68 70 72 72 73 73 75 75 76 77 77 78 79 80 84 86 86 88 95 95 98\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=score\n  /PLOT=NONE\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES\n  /CINTERVAL 95.","explain":["Tell SPSS we type the data inside the syntax, in free format, into one variable called score.","BEGIN DATA marks where the data lines start.","The 30 scores, separated by spaces. SPSS reads them in order, one per case.","END DATA marks where the data end, and the full stop finishes the command.","EXAMINE is SPSS's Explore command, which gives detailed descriptive statistics for the variable score.","Switch the plots off, so that the output is only tables.","Ask for the Descriptives table: mean, median, standard deviation and the confidence interval.","Set the confidence level to 95%, the usual choice, and end the command with a full stop."],"read":"Open the Descriptives table. Mean 74.00, Median 74.00 and Std. Deviation 12.29 describe the class. The row '95% Confidence Interval for Mean' shows a Lower Bound of 69.41 and an Upper Bound of 78.59: that is the inference.","note":"The Descriptives table shows the mean with its 95% confidence interval, the median, the standard deviation, the minimum and the maximum together."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 30\ngenerate score = real(word(\"41 57 61 62 62 62 65 65 68 68 70 72 72 73 73 75 75 76 77 77 78 79 80 84 86 86 88 95 95 98\", _n))\nsummarize score, detail\nci means score","explain":["Remove any old data from memory, so that we start from a clean sheet.","Create 30 empty rows, one for each student, ready to hold the scores.","Fill score row by row: word() picks the n-th number from the text, and real() turns it into a number. A short way to enter 30 values.","summarize with detail prints the mean, the median (the 50% row), the standard deviation and more.","ci means gives the mean with its standard error and 95% confidence interval."],"read":"In the summarize output, Mean 74 and the 50% row (the median) 74 describe the class, and Std. Dev. 12.29 is the typical spread. ci means then prints Mean 74, Std. err. 2.24 and a 95% interval of 69.41 to 78.59: the inference.","note":"The word() line is a compact way to type 30 numbers. summarize, detail gives the mean, median (the 50% row) and standard deviation (sample version). ci means gives the mean with its 95% confidence interval."},"conclusion":"The class averaged 74 with a typical spread of about 12 points: that is a plain fact about these 30 students. If they are a fair sample of similar students, the true average of that wider group plausibly lies between 69.4 and 78.6. Caution: that range is only trustworthy if the 30 students are representative of the group we want to talk about.","practice":{"scenario":"A nurse measures the resting pulse (beats per minute) of 20 volunteers at a health fair. She wants to describe these 20 people, and also to estimate the average resting pulse of all adults who might come to such fairs.","data":"pulse = [64, 70, 72, 68, 75, 80, 66, 71, 74, 69, 77, 73, 62, 78, 72, 67, 70, 76, 65, 74]","tasks":["Find the mean, median and sample standard deviation of the pulses.","Compute the standard error and a 95% confidence interval for the true mean.","Say which results describe the 20 volunteers and which one is an inference about all fair visitors."],"hint":"Follow the lab's two branches: describe with mean, median and std(ddof=1), then infer with the standard error and stats.t.interval.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\npulse = np.array([64, 70, 72, 68, 75, 80, 66, 71, 74, 69, 77, 73, 62, 78, 72, 67, 70, 76, 65, 74])\nn = len(pulse)\nmean, sd = pulse.mean(), pulse.std(ddof=1)\nse = sd / np.sqrt(n)\nlow, high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=mean, scale=se)\nprint(f'n = {n}, mean = {mean:.1f}, median = {np.median(pulse):.1f}, sd = {sd:.2f}')\nprint(f'standard error = {se:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({low:.1f}, {high:.1f})')","answer":"Mean 71.2, median 71.5 and sd 4.88: the volunteers sit about 5 beats from 71. The standard error is 1.09 and the 95% interval is 68.9 to 73.4. The first three numbers describe the 20 volunteers, while the interval infers the average of all fair visitors, assuming the volunteers are typical of them.","out":"n = 20, mean = 71.2, median = 71.5, sd = 4.88\nstandard error = 1.09\n95% CI: (68.9, 73.4)","figs":[]}}});
